# Asset-Enqueue Conventions

## Manifest-key ⇄ filename parity

Every key in `dist/manifest.json` must match the filename used in `Enqueue::getWebpackAssetUrlByKey()`.

Webpack scans `sections/*/scss/*.scss` and `sections/*/js/*.js`. Example for `section-content`:

| Asset | Key |
|---|---|
| Section CSS | `section-content-style.css` |
| Section JS (optional) | `section-content-script.js` |

Derive keys from `SECTION_SLUG`: `self::SECTION_SLUG . '-style.css'`.

`SectionReferenceTrait` already enqueues these. Do not invent extra editor CSS keys unless a `scss/[section-slug]-editor.scss` file exists.

## Guard against missing assets

```php
$sectionCss = Enqueue::getWebpackAssetUrlByKey(MANIFEST_PATH, self::SECTION_SLUG . '-style.css');
if ($sectionCss) {
    wp_enqueue_style(self::SECTION_ID . '_styles', $sectionCss);
}
```

Never enqueue an empty URL.

## Chunk naming

Webpack `/* webpackChunkName: "my-chunk" */` comments must reuse a stable slug. Built files look like `technosapiens-chunk-main-navigation.min.js`.
